  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 100 AM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in good agreement on the synoptic \nevolution through late week and into next weekend, featuring a \nlate summer Southern High Plains/Central Rockies ridge of high \npressure pattern. The Tri-State Area will sit on the northern \nperiphery of the mid level ridge axis, maintaining generally \nzonal flow aloft with subtle shortwave trough impulses ejecting \nout of Colorado on Thursday and Friday afternoons. \n\nHigh confidence exists regarding near to above normal warmth. \nThe NBM 25th to 75th percentile range for daily maximum \ntemperatures remains exceptionally tight spread of only 3-5 \ndegrees for Saturday and Sunday with temps nearing 100, \nindicating strong ensemble consensus for above normal temperatures\nthis weekend. Model spread is higher i.e. higher uncertainty for \nmaximum temperatures for Thursday and Friday on the order of 6-10 \ndegrees ranging from the upper 80s to the lower 90s depending on \ncloud cover and precipitation coverage. As the 500 mb ridge \namplifies slightly over the Southern Plains Friday into Sunday, \nwarm 850 mb thermal ridges 30-33 C build into the Plains. Median \nNBM highs push into the upper 90s for most of the CWA, with the \neastern third e.g Hill City, Norton, McCook areas seeing a 40-60% \nprobability of temperatures topping 100 degrees on Saturday and \n60-80% on Sunday for much of the area. Maximum afternoon heat \nindex values across eastern locations may approach between 100 and\n105 on Saturday and Sunday afternoons as low level moisture \nlingers east of the dryline. \n\nThursday Afternoon PoPs peak in the 20-40% range west of KS Hwy\n27 and across eastern Colorado Cheyenne/Kit Carson counties where\nterrain forced lift and low-level convergence are greatest. Later\nin the evening model forecasts indicate higher probabilities \nheading into Thursday night with increasing chances of at least \nsome precipitation with model averages between 0.15 and 0.25, \nlocally higher amounts possible as high as 40% chance of \nexceeding 0.30. Ensemble SBCAPE probabilities exceed 40% for > \n1000 J/kg east of the dryline each afternoon. However, deep-layer \nshear remains modest 20-25 kts, keeping storms mostly somewhat \nunorganized at this time. High inverted-V low levels DCAPE > 1200\nJ/kg suggest localized strong downburst winds 40-60 mph will be \nthe primary threats with collapsing cells.",
